<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">North Macedonia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of North Macedonia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">North Macedonia is a landlocked country in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a>. Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> until 1912, the region was divided after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a>, and its northern part became a republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in 1944. It declared independence in 1991 and took the name North Macedonia in 2019 under the Prespa Agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Both it and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> claim the heritage of the 1903 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilinden-preobrazhenie-uprising">Ilinden Uprising</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-macedonia.jpg" alt="North Macedonia" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SKopp, redrawn by Gabbe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Macedonia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greece has the largest economy in the Balkans and is an important regional investor: the second foreign investor in Albania and the largest in <b>North Macedonia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hellenic-republic">Hellenic Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…to the Ottoman Empire from the Strandzha Mountains; in Bosnia it is a sevdalinka and a qasida; Albania, Serbia (a recording of 1910), <b>North Macedonia</b> and Hungary have their own, and Greece knows it under at least eight names, among them &quot;Little trehantiri&quot; and &quot;From a foreign place&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/katibim">Kâtibim (Üsküdar'a Gider İken)</a></li><li><blockquote>The day is kept as Alphabet Day in Albania, Kosovo and <b>North Macedonia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-22">22 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Bulgaria and <b>North Macedonia</b> both claim the uprising, and the Ilinden memorial at Kruševo is a national symbol of North Macedonia.</blockquote><a href="/state/krusevo-republic">Kruševo Republic</a></li></ul></section>
